  "notes": {
    "raw": "\\*Data above 298 K estimated except temperature and enthalpy of fusion. \nPhase changes: 1113 K, melting point of $GeS_{2}$ ; $\\Delta H^{\\circ} = 3.895$ kcal/mol.\n1210.4 K, melting point of Ge; $\\Delta H^{\\circ} = 8.830$ kcal/mol. \nHeat capacity (cal/mol·K) and enthalpy (kcal/mol) equations: \n<table><tr><td>298.15-1113 K:</td><td> $Cp^o$  = 15.665 + 2.624x $10^{-3}$ T - 0.664x $10^5$ T $^{-2}$ </td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"3\">1113-1500 K:</td><td> $H^o-H_2^o_{98}$  = 15.665x $10^{-3}$ T + 1.312x $10^{-6}$ T $^2$  + 0.664x $10^2$ T $^{-1}$  - 5.010</td></tr><tr><td> $Cp^o$  = 23.900</td></tr><tr><td> $H^o-H_2^o_{98}$  = 23.900x $10^{-3}$ T - 8.596</td></tr></table>\nReaction equations (kcal/mol): \n<table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">298.15-1113 K:</td><td> $\\Delta Hr^{\\circ} = -60.986 + 1.741 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} + 0.526 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} - 26.800 \\text{T}^{-1}$ </td></tr><tr><td> $\\Delta Gr^{\\circ} = -60.986 - 1.741 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} \\ln \\text{T} - 0.526 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} - 13.400 \\text{T}^{-1} + 51.389 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T}$ </td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">1113-1210.4 K:</td><td> $\\Delta Hr^{\\circ} = -64.572 + 9.976 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} - 0.786 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} - 93.200 \\text{T}^{-1}$ </td></tr><tr><td> $\\Delta Gr^{\\circ} = -64.572 - 9.976 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} \\ln \\text{T} + 0.786 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} - 46.600 \\text{T}^{-1} + 110.945 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T}$ </td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">1210.4-1500 K:</td><td> $\\Delta Hr^{\\circ} = -72.871 + 8.957 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} - 0.320 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} - 68.300 \\text{T}^{-1}$ </td></tr><tr><td> $\\Delta Gr^{\\circ} = -72.871 - 8.957 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T} \\ln \\text{T} + 0.320 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{T}^{2} - 34.150 \\text{T}^{-1} + 111.124 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{T}$ </td></tr></table>\nSources: Enthalpy of formation at 298 K from Murray (84). Other data from Gurvich (49) who estimated all above 298 K except temperature and enthalpy of fusion. ",
